	<item>
		<title><![CDATA[TB-500 and Thymosin β4: What the Names Actually Refer To]]></title>
		<link>https://example.com/tb-500-thymosin-beta-4-literature/</link>
		<pubDate>Mon, 14 Sep 2026 22:40:16 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://example.com/?p=1009</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>This is the single most useful thing to understand about this material, and most listings get it wrong.</p>

<p><strong>Thymosin β4 (Tβ4)</strong> is a 43-residue protein found naturally in many cell types, involved in actin sequestration. It has an extensive literature going back decades and has been through clinical trials for several indications.</p>

<p><strong>TB-500</strong> is the name commonly used in the research-chemical market for a <strong>short synthetic fragment</strong> of that protein — usually the seven-residue actin-binding region, sometimes acetylated. It is not thymosin β4. It is a fragment of it.</p>

<p>Those are different molecules with different molecular weights, different synthesis routes and — critically — different literatures. A vendor page that lists "TB-500" and then cites thymosin β4 clinical work is attaching one molecule's evidence to another molecule's product. Check the sequence on the certificate, not the name on the label.</p>

<h2>What each literature covers</h2>

<p><strong>Thymosin β4 (the full protein):</strong></p>

<ul><li>Actin sequestration and cytoskeletal dynamics, which is the established biochemistry.</li><li>Corneal and dermal wound models.</li><li>Cardiac injury models.</li><li>Human clinical trials, including for dry eye and epidermolysis bullosa — these exist and are findable, and they concern the full protein.</li></ul>

<p><strong>The short fragment sold as TB-500:</strong></p>

<ul><li>A much thinner literature, largely in-vitro, focused on cell migration endpoints.</li><li>Frequently studied alongside, rather than instead of, the full protein.</li></ul>

<p>The asymmetry is the point. If a claim rests on clinical data, it almost certainly rests on Tβ4 data, and the fragment in the vial is not the molecule that was tested.</p>

<h2>What neither literature establishes</h2>

<ul><li>No approval by any major regulator for the fragment, for any indication.</li><li>No published human efficacy data for the short fragment.</li><li>Fragment-to-protein extrapolation is an assumption, not a finding. A sequence that binds actin in isolation does not necessarily reproduce the behaviour of the protein it came from.</li></ul>

<h2>Checking what you actually received</h2>

<p>The certificate should state the <strong>sequence</strong> and a <strong>theoretical mass</strong> consistent with it. A seven-residue acetylated fragment and a 43-residue protein differ by thousands of daltons — this is the easiest identity check in the entire catalog, and it takes thirty seconds with any peptide mass calculator. The <a href="/mass-spectrometry-identity-confirmation/">mass spectrometry guide</a> walks through it.</p>

<p>If the certificate names only "TB-500" with no sequence, you cannot tell which molecule you are holding. Ask before ordering.</p>

<p>Supplied as a reference material for laboratory research use only. Not for human or veterinary use.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[TB-500 and thymosin β4 are not the same molecule, and the literature attached to one is routinely used to sell the other. Here is the distinction.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>1009</wp:post_id>
